      "name": null,
      "problem": "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $100{x}^{2}=7.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ 100{x}^{2}-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ (10x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (10x-\\sqrt{7})(10x+\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n10x-\\sqrt{7}=0\\,\\text{or}\\,10x+\\sqrt{7}=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\sqrt{7} & =0 & \\, & \\, & 10x+\\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ 10x & =\\sqrt{7} & \\, & \\, & 10x & =-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$",
